(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-female-reproductive-quiz-version-3"]={"title":"Female Reproductive System — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 3)","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The paired embryonic ducts that give rise to the major internal female reproductive organs (in the absence of AMH) are the:","choices":["Mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts","Paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts","Pronephric embryonic ducts","Metanephric collecting ducts"],"answer":1,"exp":"The paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts develop into the internal female reproductive organs when anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH) is absent to trigger their regression. — Slide 16 (The Müllerian ducts)"},{"q":"The fused caudal portions of the Müllerian ducts form the uterovaginal primordium, which gives rise to the uterus, cervix, and:","choices":["Upper one-third of the vagina","Lower two-thirds of vagina","Fallopian (uterine) tubes","Ovarian follicular tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"The fused caudal Müllerian ducts form the uterus, cervix, and upper third of the vagina; the lower vagina arises separately from the urogenital sinus. — Slide 16 (The Müllerian ducts)"},{"q":"The unfused cranial portions of the paramesonephric ducts differentiate into the:","choices":["Ovarian cortex and stroma","Fallopian (uterine) tubes","Uterine cervix canal","Labia majora skin folds"],"answer":1,"exp":"The unfused cranial portions of the Müllerian ducts become the fallopian (uterine) tubes. — Slides 16–17 (Müllerian system)"},{"q":"The perineum is a diamond-shaped area bounded anteriorly by the pubic symphysis, laterally by the ischial tuberosities, and posteriorly by the:","choices":["Coccyx","Sacral promontory","Iliac crest","Pubic tubercle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The perineum is circumscribed anteriorly by the pubic symphysis, laterally by the ischial tuberosities, and posteriorly by the coccyx. — Slide 5 (Perineum)"},{"q":"The anterior (urogenital) triangle of the female perineum contains which structures?","choices":["The ischial spines bilaterally","The anus and anal triangle","The coccygeal apex region","The urethral and vaginal orifices"],"answer":3,"exp":"The anterior (urogenital) triangle contains the urethral and vaginal orifices in females; the posterior (anal) triangle contains the anus. — Slide 5 (Perineum)"},{"q":"The imaginary line dividing the perineum into its anterior and posterior triangles runs between the:","choices":["Ischial tuberosities","Pubic tubercles","Anterior superior iliac spines","Sacroiliac joints"],"answer":0,"exp":"A horizontal line between the ischial tuberosities forms the shared base of the two perineal triangles. — Slide 5 (Perineum)"},{"q":"The double fold of peritoneum that attaches the ovary to the broad ligament is the:","choices":["Cardinal ligament","Round ligament","Suspensory ligament","Mesovarium"],"answer":3,"exp":"The mesovarium is a double peritoneal fold attaching the ovary to the broad ligament. — Slide 14 (Peritoneal Folds / Ligaments)"},{"q":"The suspensory ligament of the ovary connects the ovary to the:","choices":["Pelvic (lateral body) wall","Uterine fundus surface","Labia majora tissue fold","Sacral promontory ridge"],"answer":0,"exp":"The suspensory ligament runs from the ovary/mesovarium to the pelvic (lateral body) wall and carries the ovarian vessels. — Slides 14, 18 (Ligaments)"},{"q":"The round ligament of the uterus travels from the uterus through which structure to reach the labia majora?","choices":["The greater sciatic foramen","The obturator foramen","The inguinal canal","The femoral canal"],"answer":2,"exp":"The round ligament extends from the uterus through the inguinal canal to the labia majora, helping maintain uterine anteversion. — Slides 18, 36 (Round ligament)"},{"q":"Which fold of peritoneum drapes over the uterus and also holds the ovaries and uterine tubes?","choices":["Round ligament","Ovarian ligament","Broad ligament","Uterosacral ligament"],"answer":2,"exp":"The broad ligament is a peritoneal fold draping over the uterus and extending to hold the ovaries and uterine tubes. — Slides 14, 18 (Broad ligament)"},{"q":"The pelvic diaphragm is composed of the coccygeus and which other muscle group?","choices":["Obturator externus","Rectus abdominis","Bulbospongiosus","Levator ani"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pelvic diaphragm comprises the levator ani and coccygeus muscles, which support the pelvic organs and seal the pelvic outlet. — Slide 9 (Pelvic Diaphragm)"},{"q":"The levator ani muscle is made up of the puborectalis, iliococcygeus, and:","choices":["Bulbospongiosus","Coccygeus","Pubococcygeus","Ischiocavernosus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The levator ani consists of the puborectalis, pubococcygeus, and iliococcygeus. — Slide 9 (Pelvic Diaphragm)"},{"q":"A key function of the pelvic diaphragm is to:","choices":["Form the epithelial lining of the vaginal birth canal","Produce oocytes through monthly follicular maturation","Secrete cervical mucus that changes with the cycle","Support pelvic organs and seal the inferior opening of the bony pelvis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pelvic diaphragm supports the pelvic organs, seals the inferior pelvic opening, and lifts to help release feces during defecation. — Slide 9 (Pelvic Diaphragm)"},{"q":"The ovarian artery, which supplies the ovary, arises from which vessel?","choices":["The internal iliac artery","The abdominal aorta","The uterine artery","The internal pudendal artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ovarian artery arises from the abdominal aorta (inferior to the renal arteries) and reaches the ovary via the suspensory ligament. — Slides 24, 27, 41 (Vasculature of Ovaries)"},{"q":"The uterine artery, a major supply to the uterus, is a branch of which vessel?","choices":["External iliac artery","Abdominal aorta","Internal iliac artery","Ovarian artery"],"answer":2,"exp":"The uterine arteries arise from the internal iliac arteries; the ovarian arteries come from the aorta. — Slide 41 (The Uterine Blood Supply)"},{"q":"Venous drainage of the ovaries is asymmetric: the right ovarian vein drains into the IVC, while the left drains into the:","choices":["Right azygos vein","Hepatic portal vein","Left splenic vein","Left renal vein"],"answer":3,"exp":"The right ovarian vein drains to the IVC; the left ovarian vein drains to the left renal vein. — Slide 24 (Ovary)"},{"q":"Parasympathetic innervation to the ovaries is carried by which nerves?","choices":["Vagus nerve","Pelvic splanchnic nerves","Phrenic nerve","Greater splanchnic nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ovaries receive parasympathetic supply from the pelvic splanchnic nerves; visceral afferents enter the cord at T10–T11. — Slide 27 (Innervation of Ovaries)"},{"q":"Visceral afferent (pain) fibers from the ovary enter the spinal cord at which levels?","choices":["T10 and T11","L4 and L5","S2, S3, S4","C6 and C7"],"answer":0,"exp":"Afferent fibers from the ovary enter the spinal cord through the T10 and T11 nerves, explaining periumbilical referred pain. — Slide 27 (Innervation of Ovaries)"},{"q":"Nerve supply to the uterine tube is derived from which plexi?","choices":["Celiac and mesenteric plexi","Cardiac and pulmonary plexi","Ovarian and uterine plexi","Cervical and brachial plexi"],"answer":2,"exp":"The uterine tube receives nerve supply from the ovarian and uterine plexi. — Slide 48 (Uterine Tube)"},{"q":"Which sequence lists the regions of the uterus from superior to the vaginal end?","choices":["Fundus → body → isthmus → cervix","Cervix → isthmus → body → fundus","Body → fundus → cervix → isthmus","Isthmus → fundus → body → cervix"],"answer":0,"exp":"From superior to inferior: fundus, body, isthmus (constricted region), then cervix, which joins the vagina. — Slides 33–34 (Regions of the Uterus)"},{"q":"The uterus normally sits in which position relative to the urinary bladder and rectum?","choices":["Posterior to both the bladder and rectum entirely","Between the urinary bladder (anterior) and rectum (posterior)","Anterior to both the bladder and rectum entirely","Superior to the kidneys within the upper abdominal cavity"],"answer":1,"exp":"The uterus lies between the urinary bladder anteriorly and the rectum posteriorly. — Slide 32 (Uterus)"},{"q":"The uterine tube region into which the fimbriae open, receiving the ovulated oocyte, is the:","choices":["Interstitial (intramural) segment","Isthmus","Infundibulum","Ampulla"],"answer":2,"exp":"The infundibulum is the flared, fimbriated distal end of the uterine tube that receives the ovulated oocyte. — Slides 45, 51 (Uterine Tube)"},{"q":"The vagina in an adult female measures approximately how long?","choices":["About 25 cm","About 10 cm","About 3 cm","About 18 cm"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vagina is a thick-walled fibromuscular tube about 10 cm long, forming the inferior-most region of the tract. — Slide 56 (Vagina)"},{"q":"The uterine tubes are lined by which epithelium, whose cilia help move the oocyte toward the uterus?","choices":["Simple squamous endothelium","Stratified squamous epithelium","Transitional urothelial epithelium","Simple ciliated columnar epithelium"],"answer":3,"exp":"The uterine tube mucosa is simple ciliated (and non-ciliated) columnar epithelium; ciliary action and peristalsis move the ovum. — Slides 45, 53 (Uterine Tube Histology)"},{"q":"The middle tunic of the uterine wall, composed of three intertwining layers of smooth muscle, is the:","choices":["Endometrium","Perimetrium","Myometrium","Adventitia"],"answer":2,"exp":"The myometrium is the thick middle tunic of smooth muscle; it enlarges greatly in pregnancy. — Slides 35, 39 (Uterine Layers)"},{"q":"The functional unit of the ovary, a sac-like structure containing an oocyte surrounded by follicular cells, is the:","choices":["Ovarian follicle","Corpus luteum only","Uterine gland","Fornix"],"answer":0,"exp":"Ovaries are composed of ovarian follicles, each containing an oocyte and its surrounding follicular cells. — Slides 25–26 (Ovaries)"},{"q":"The anterior peritoneal recess between the uterus and the urinary bladder is the:","choices":["Mesovarium peritoneal fold","Rectouterine (Douglas) pouch","Broad ligament peritoneal fold","Vesicouterine pouch"],"answer":3,"exp":"The vesicouterine pouch is the anterior dead-end recess between the uterus and bladder. — Slide 12 (Anatomy of the Female Reproductive System)"},{"q":"The outer serosal tunic of the uterus, continuous with the broad ligament, is the:","choices":["Mucosa","Myometrium","Endometrium","Perimetrium"],"answer":3,"exp":"The perimetrium is the outer serosa of the uterus and is continuous with the broad ligament. — Slide 39 (Wall of the Uterus)"},{"q":"The greater vestibular gland, which secretes mucus into the vestibule, is homologous to which male structure?","choices":["The prostate gland tissue","The bulbo-urethral (Cowper's) gland","The paired seminal vesicle glands","The epididymal duct system"],"answer":1,"exp":"The greater vestibular gland lies behind the bulb of the vestibule and is homologous to the male bulbo-urethral gland. — Slide 78 (Greater Vestibular Gland)"},{"q":"The clitoris, located where the labia minora meet anteriorly, contains erectile tissue and is homologous to which male organ?","choices":["The testis","The scrotum","The penis","The prostate"],"answer":2,"exp":"The clitoris contains erectile tissue (two corpora cavernosa and two crura) and is homologous to the penis. — Slides 71, 76 (Clitoris)"}]};
